"Black Holes and Lensing | VirtualDemos",
"Black Holes and Lensing",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (Einstein’s theory of gravity: black holes, light bending and gravitational time dilation). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Einstein ring; Two images; Microlensing event; Close to a stellar black hole; Sagittarius A*; M87*.",
"Three views. <strong>Lensing</strong> shows a source seen through a point mass lens, with its light curve and a graph of where its images sit. <strong>Ray traced</strong> is the view of an observer hovering near a black hole, with every light path computed exactly. <strong>Near the hole</strong> shows an observer hovering at a distance \\(r\\) from the center, with that observer’s clock, the light they send outward and the tidal stretch on a {#} m person.",
"In Lensing, the source moves along a straight track past the lens. The light curve’s time axis is in units of the Einstein crossing time \\(t_E\\), the time to cross one Einstein radius at {#} km/s; the time marker on the light curve, and the source itself, can be dragged.",
"The lens view is a thin lens with a point mass and small angles, using ordinary distances (cosmology would use angular diameter distances). The microlensing source star is drawn far bigger than a real star so its images can be seen; the “point source” magnification is for a source of zero size, and the “this source” value is for the disk as drawn.",
"Near the hole and Ray traced use a Schwarzschild (not rotating) black hole. Clock rates and redshifts are for an observer hovering at fixed \\(r\\), and are exact. The tidal stretch is for a person falling feet first, small compared with \\(r\\).",
"Record reading stores what the readouts of the open view show. In Lensing: the setup, lens mass, distances, Einstein radius, crossing time, the time from closest approach (the source’s position along its track times \\(t_E\\)) and the magnifications. Near the hole: the mass, \\(r_s\\), the distance in km and in \\(r_s\\), the clock rate, \\(1 + z\\), and the tidal stretch here and at the horizon. Ray traced: the observer’s distance, the shadow’s radius, its far away estimate, and the Einstein ring’s radius, exact and by the thin lens formula. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the matching data table below (Lensing readings with one lens mass go to the light curve table); Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Use three predictions of general relativity to measure a black hole and a lens: the bending of light (the Einstein radius and a microlensing light curve), the slowing of clocks near a mass, and the shadow a black hole casts on the sky behind it.",
"<strong>The Schwarzschild radius.</strong> A mass \\(M\\) squeezed inside",
"\\[r_s = \\frac{2GM}{c^2}\\]",
"is a black hole: nothing, not even light, gets out from inside \\(r_s\\) (§{#}).",
"<strong>Light bending and the Einstein radius.</strong> Light passing a mass at a distance \\(b\\) much larger than \\(r_s\\) is bent by an angle \\(2r_s/b\\). A source exactly behind a lens at distance \\(D_L\\), with the source at \\(D_S\\), appears as a ring of angular radius \\(\\theta_E\\), where",
"\\[\\theta_E^2 = 2r_s\\,\\frac{D_S - D_L}{D_L D_S} = 2r_s\\left(\\frac{1}{D_L} - \\frac{1}{D_S}\\right).\\]",
"A source off to the side by \\(u\\) (in units of \\(\\theta_E\\)) makes two images, together brighter than the source alone by",
"\\[A(u) = \\frac{u^2 + 2}{u\\sqrt{u^2 + 4}}.\\]",
"<strong>A microlensing event.</strong> A star passing in front of a more distant star makes images only about a milliarcsecond apart, far too close to see as two. What a telescope sees is one star that brightens and fades. If the source moves past the lens in a straight line, with closest approach \\(u_0\\) at time \\(t_0\\), then",
"\\[u^2 = u_0^2 + \\left(\\frac{t - t_0}{t_E}\\right)^2,\\]",
"where \\(t_E\\) is the time to cross one Einstein radius: \\(t_E = \\theta_E D_L/v\\) for a speed \\(v\\) across the line of sight.",
"<strong>Clocks and light near a black hole.</strong> A clock hovering at a distance \\(r\\) from the center ticks slower than a clock far away by the factor \\(\\sqrt{1 - r_s/r}\\), and light sent outward from it arrives stretched by \\(1 + z = 1/\\sqrt{1 - r_s/r}\\). The difference in gravity across a body of length \\(L\\) falling feet first is the tidal stretch \\(\\Delta A = 2GML/r^3\\).",
"<strong>The shadow.</strong> Light coming in with an impact parameter less than \\(b_c = (\\sqrt{27}/2)\\,r_s\\) falls in. An observer hovering at \\(r_o\\) sees a dark disk, the shadow, of angular radius \\(\\alpha\\) with",
"\\[\\sin\\alpha = \\frac{b_c}{r_o}\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{r_s}{r_o}}\\qquad (r_o \\ge 1.5\\,r_s).\\]",
"Far away this becomes \\(\\sin\\alpha \\approx b_c/r_o\\) (the demo’s far away estimate), and \\(\\alpha \\approx b_c/r_o\\) once the angle is small.",
